\SMT{} solvers are a cornerstone of modern computer science, providing foundational reasoning for applications from formal verification to program analysis~\cite{demoura2008z3, ball2004slam}. While these solvers have become powerful, their performance often degrades sharply when faced with complex, real-world constraints. The root of this fragility lies less in computational power than in search: general-purpose branching and propagation heuristics receive little guidance about which variables and relations are most consequential.
They explore vast search spaces without a high-level understanding of a formula's structure or the critical role of specific variables, a limitation that is the primary source of the infamous constraint-solving bottleneck~\cite{cadar2013symbolic}. 
Specifically, the issue is not merely that heuristics are ``insufficient'', but that search lacks goal-directed signals about where to invest effort. Branching and propagation receive little guidance on which variables and relations are structurally pivotal for progress, inflating case splits and weakening propagation under tight time budgets. This guidance gap—rather than raw compute—accounts for unstable performance on real-world constraints and frequent timeouts on difficult benchmarks. \wyc{This part is not very necessary, since previous sentences have already introduced the major issues of constraint solving.}


Consider a typical program analysis scenario with a complex non-linear guard. A standard solver may exhaust its budget on \wyc{complex but} irrelevant \wyc{what is irrelevant assignment? do you mean non-critical variables?} assignments, while an expert quickly identifies a high-impact variable. Existing simplification relies on shallow heuristics and lacks the semantic context needed for strategic transformation \wyc{what is strategic transformation?}. 
In contrast, representative systems have largely focused on scalable path exploration and input guidance (\eg{}, S2E and Mayhem~\cite{chipounov2011s2e, avgerinos2012mayhem}; Driller/QSYM for hybrid fuzzing~\cite{DBLP:conf/uss/StephensPCE16, yunQSYMPracticalConcolic2018}), together with compiler-level or binary-level symbolic execution frameworks (\eg{}, SymCC and SymQEMU~\cite{poeplauSymbolicExecutionSymCC2020, poeplauSymQEMUCompilationbased2021}) and adaptive tuning and ML-guided complexity modeling (e.g., SymTuner and FeatMaker~\cite{chaSymTunerMaximizingPower2022, yoon2024featmaker}). 
\wyc{In contrast, existing methods largely focus on three aspects: (1) scalable path exploration and input guidance (\eg{}, S2E~\cite{chipounov2011s2e}, Mayhem~\cite{avgerinos2012mayhem}); (2) compiler-level or binary-level symbolic execution frameworks (\eg{}, SymCC~\cite{poeplauSymbolicExecutionSymCC2020} and SymQEMU~\cite{poeplauSymQEMUCompilationbased2021}); (3) adaptive tuning and ML-guided complexity modeling (\eg, SymTuner~\cite{chaSymTunerMaximizingPower2022} and FeatMaker~\cite{yoon2024featmaker}).} \wyc{A more critical issue: why do I care about these works, are they related to the content discussed before?}
While these directions have advanced exploration and infrastructure, they generally do not deliver semantically informed reduction of constraint complexity. Moreover, rewrite-based normalization (\eg{}, equality saturation~\cite{willsey2021egg}) emphasizes canonicalization rather than goal-directed, variable-level concretization, revealing a core challenge: solvers and pre-processors lack variable-level semantic awareness and learned policies for where and how to simplify under tight budgets.


