To address this limitation, we propose \tool (\textbf{CO}llaborative \textbf{M}ethod with \textbf{P}olicy-learning \textbf{A}nd \textbf{S}emantic \textbf{S}ynthesis)\footnote{Analogous to a compass—where the needle (\RL{}) sets the strategic direction and the dial (\LLM{}) provides tactical detail—\tool integrates these roles to guide \SMT{} solvers efficiently through vast and complex search spaces.}, a framework that unifies \RL{} for deciding \emph{what} to simplify with \LLM{}-guided semantic synthesis for deciding \emph{how}, all under solver-in-the-loop verification. 
Specifically,  \tool simplifies SMT constraints through a solver-in-the-loop pipeline with three stages.  First, it canonicalizes formulas to remove naming variance and encodes them into vector representation. Second, a dual-agent policy decomposes simplification: a \RL agent strategically selects high-impact variables for concretization, while a \LLM{} proposes semantically guided values. Finally, a hybrid reward system—combining solver feedback, predictive models, and history-based penalties—drives policy optimization.  



% Our framework decomposes simplification into two components: a strategic \RL{} agent that decides what to simplify (variable selection), and a tactical \LLM{} that determines how (value suggestion). The \RL{} agent targets high-impact variables to prune the search; the \LLM{} provides context-aware assignments verified by the solver. Much like a real compass—where the needle (policy learning via \RL{}) sets the strategic direction and the dial (semantic synthesis via \LLM{}) provides the tactical details—\tool synergizes \RL{} and \LLM{} to guide \SMT{} solvers through vast and complex search spaces.

